WEBVTT

00:01.580 --> 00:06.600
In U.S. vs. Hepner, a federal judge ruled that consumer chat GPT for legal research

00:06.600 --> 00:08.500
can waive attorney client privilege.

00:09.700 --> 00:12.700
The model logs your queries, stores them, and trains on them.

00:13.000 --> 00:13.980
That's not privileged communication.

00:14.820 --> 00:17.440
That's actually a public filing you didn't even know you made.

00:23.400 --> 00:28.360
Consumer AI logs your queries, trains on your data, and then shares it with third parties.

00:29.000 --> 00:32.040
Enterprise AI keeps everything inside your own walls.

00:32.620 --> 00:36.420
If you're using AI without knowing which kind, you're not making a decision.

00:37.040 --> 00:38.920
You're making a gamble with client's data.

00:42.140 --> 00:45.000
Judge Rakoff didn't ban AI from legal work.

00:45.520 --> 00:46.380
He just drew a line.

00:46.900 --> 00:51.360
If your AI tools, terms of service, allow data retention, sharing, or model training,

00:51.820 --> 00:54.400
anything you type into it is no longer protected.

00:54.940 --> 00:58.680
And 69% of lawyers use consumer AI tools right now.

00:59.380 --> 01:01.260
Most of them have never read the terms of service.

01:01.720 --> 01:02.920
That's not a technology problem.

01:03.440 --> 01:04.560
That's a governance failure.

01:09.500 --> 01:13.720
Before you put another client's question in the chat GPT, read the terms of service.

01:14.240 --> 01:16.660
Not the marketing page, the actual terms.

01:17.260 --> 01:21.240
If it says, we may use your inputs to improve our models, congratulations.

01:21.800 --> 01:24.380
You just published your client's privileged communication.

01:29.680 --> 01:32.820
LexisNexis AI hallucinates 70% of the time.

01:33.700 --> 01:35.340
Westlaw's tool? 34%.

01:36.120 --> 01:37.760
These aren't experimental toys.

01:38.460 --> 01:41.380
These are tools lawyers are citing in court filings right now.

01:42.400 --> 01:47.040
Verify every citation you need to, every single one.

01:52.000 --> 01:56.140
You wouldn't let a first year associate file a brief without checking every citation, would you?

01:56.800 --> 01:59.760
So why are you letting an AI model do exactly that?

02:00.520 --> 02:06.120
With a hallucination rate north of 70%, the tool doesn't replace the due diligence you need.

02:06.840 --> 02:07.680
It adds to it.

02:14.200 --> 02:20.660
Private RAG means your AI searches your own briefs, your own motions, your own case history,

02:21.240 --> 02:23.420
the data never leaves your infrastructure.

02:24.140 --> 02:25.700
No third party terms of service apply.

02:26.380 --> 02:27.640
No privilege questions exist.

02:28.280 --> 02:31.080
Public AI means your data goes to someone else's server,

02:31.720 --> 02:34.500
gets logged, and maybe gets used to train the next model.

02:35.740 --> 02:38.180
Same search, completely different liability.

02:44.220 --> 02:46.740
When I have conversations around AI with newbies,

02:46.900 --> 02:49.820
I always get the question, which AI tool should I use?

02:50.300 --> 02:51.380
And that's the wrong question.

02:51.960 --> 02:55.520
The right question is, where does my data go after I press enter?

02:56.000 --> 03:00.620
Or what tools do I need to give my AI in order for it to be efficient?

03:01.380 --> 03:05.900
The architecture behind it determines whether you actually have privilege or lose it,

03:06.180 --> 03:08.740
or whether AI works or it doesn't.

03:12.400 --> 03:17.320
In 2025 and 2026, lawyers have been sanctioned over $100,000

03:17.980 --> 03:22.880
for AI misused in court filings, fake citations, hallucinated case law,

03:23.200 --> 03:25.320
unverified outputs presented as fact.

03:26.200 --> 03:30.260
The courts aren't anti-AI, they're anti-careless, there's a difference.

03:35.180 --> 03:39.200
Most legal malpractice policies have an exclusion you've never read.

03:39.880 --> 03:42.980
If the error was caused by a tool you used without proper verification,

03:42.980 --> 03:47.600
and you can't prove you checked, coverage gets complicated fast.

03:48.240 --> 03:51.520
AI isn't the risk, skipping the verification step, those.

03:56.420 --> 04:00.420
Nearly 47% of law firms have zero AI governance policy.

04:01.180 --> 04:06.700
No usage guidelines, no approval tool list, no audit trail, and that's not a gap.

04:07.300 --> 04:09.140
That's an open door to a malpractice claim.

04:09.990 --> 04:12.120
The fix? It's not even that complicated.

04:13.140 --> 04:16.280
Just have an AI governance policy that covers all of this.

04:17.020 --> 04:18.200
It's not optional anymore.

04:22.920 --> 04:28.920
If your firm doesn't have an AI policy, every attorney who uses AI is making up their own rules,

04:29.280 --> 04:30.340
every single one.

04:31.560 --> 04:32.540
That's not innovation.

04:33.500 --> 04:37.060
That's uncontrolled risk distributed across every desk in the building.

04:42.800 --> 04:46.160
I built my software because the head no ruling showed me what was coming.

04:46.720 --> 04:50.660
Law firms need AI that search their own work, not the internet's work,

04:51.280 --> 04:56.520
trained on their own briefs, their motions, their outcomes, no third-party data retention,

04:57.180 --> 05:00.740
no privileged questions, the research stays inside your walls.

05:01.260 --> 05:03.640
That's not a feature, that's the entire point.

05:07.820 --> 05:10.400
I'm not building the software because the market told me to.

05:10.900 --> 05:13.760
I'm building it because I watch firms expose privileged data

05:13.760 --> 05:16.300
using tools that weren't designed for them.

05:16.780 --> 05:19.100
The technology exists to do this right.

05:19.780 --> 05:22.340
Someone just needs to build it for the people that actually need it,

05:22.580 --> 05:23.500
and that's what I'm doing.

